  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nQuiet and dry conditions will persist through at least early\nSunday under high pressure. Following the recent cold frontal \npassage, highs today will be near to slightly below average. \nThe combination of radiational cooling tonight and northerly \nflow will allow lows to fall into the mid-30s for parts of the \nregion. Frost potential is slightly elevated across the ridges \nand areas northeast of Pittsburgh (near I-80); Frost Advisory \nremains in effect from midnight to 9am Tuesday. A strengthening \nsouthwest flow later this week will returner warm temperatures \nand effectively end the frost threat into the weekend. \n\nThe next chance for rain will depend on the development and\ntrack of a potential tropical disturbance. The National \nHurricane Center gives the system a 70% chance of formation over\nthe next seven days in the southwest Gulf. Given the noted\nuncertainty, rain chances range from 20-50% Sunday through \nTuesday. Some model solutions have little to no rainfall if the\nsystem fizzles out and/or tracks east before venture north \ntowards the Ohio River Valley. However, there are a few \nensembles tracking the remnants of the disturbance into portions\nof our region and totals could exceed an inch. Temperature\nforecast carry similar uncertainty overt the weekend. Drier \nscenarios support highs in the low to mid-70s, while wetter \nsolutions favor highs in the low to mid-60s.",
